1|0@wma^1439925094#cu7d7ht:Jetman123$:"Langes messers" and falchions are the same thing, the weapons just look a little different because they come from different cultures. I don't think a historical person would have rigidly defined the term as we do now, focusing overmuch on the specific shape of the guard. The Germans called their falchions "long knives/cutters". Langes messer means "long knife", with messer in this context summoning the image of the type of knife you cut meat with. "Chopper" is definitely the type of word you would use in informal english. The English took their word root from the Latin "falx", sickle/chopper, getting "falxion/falchion". -ion in Latin is a suffix that denotes action, so a "falx-ion" is a scyther/reaper/cutter. "Chopper" is again the type of informal word this brings to mind. Any actual professional linguists hanging around can feel free to correct me, but from what I can tell, translating "messer" in this context as "falchion" is entirely reasonable.
